+            <p>Assuming F1 to F4, let \(A\) be real symmetric with eigenvalues \(\lambda_j\) listed so that \(\lambda_1\) is the largest, and with the orthonormal eigenbasis \(q_1, \ldots, q_n\) of Theorem 2.3. Prove that \(\max\{x^{\top}Ax : \|x\| = 1\} = \lambda_1\), and that the maximum is attained at \(q_1\).</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
               <p>Under Assumptions F1 to F4, let \(\|x\| = 1\) and \(y = Q^{\top}x\). Then \(\|y\|^2 = x^{\top}QQ^{\top}x = x^{\top}x = 1\), using \(QQ^{\top} = I\) from Lemma 1.4, so \(\sum_j y_j^2 = 1\). Under Assumptions F1 to F4, Corollary 2.5 gives \(x^{\top}Ax = \sum_j \lambda_j y_j^2 \le \lambda_1 \sum_j y_j^2 = \lambda_1\). For \(x = q_1\), \(y\) is the first standard basis vector \(e_1\), and the value is \(\lambda_1\). So the maximum is \(\lambda_1\), attained at \(q_1\).</p>
