+            <p>Assuming F1 to F4 of this lesson, for a real symmetric \(A = Q\Lambda Q^{\top}\) (Theorem 2.3) and a real number \(\mu\), show that \(A - \mu I\) has the same eigenvectors as \(A\), with eigenvalues \(\lambda_j - \mu\).</p>
             <details class="solution"><summary>Solution</summary>
               <p>Since \(QQ^{\top} = I\) (Lemma 1.4, under Assumption F1), \(A - \mu I = Q\Lambda Q^{\top} - \mu QQ^{\top} = Q(\Lambda - \mu I)Q^{\top}\). Hence \((A - \mu I)q_j = Q(\Lambda - \mu I)e_j = (\lambda_j - \mu)q_j\). The columns \(q_j\) are the same eigenvectors, and the eigenvalues are shifted by \(\mu\).</p>
             </details>
